+### 7.3 Target-independent negative control
+
+For
+
+```text
+g=y^2-x^2-x,
+tau_+ = (x^2+x+c_2)^(1/2),    tau_-=-tau_+,
+```
+
+the coefficients of `x^-1=t^1` in `1/g_y(tau_+)` and
+`1/g_y(tau_-)` are `+1/2` and `-1/2`.  The failure is independent of the
+target, and the wrapper reports exactly `failure_order_t=1`.
+
+### 7.4 Vacuity mutation and saturation control
+
+For the passing cubic shear take `x=z^-3`, let
+`omega=(-1+sqrt(3)I)/2`, and index the exact roots as
+
+```text
+tau_j=-omega^(-j)z^-1,       j=0,1,2.
+```
+
+Mutate one **independent** tame slot at exponent `z^1`, carrying its conjugates
+with it:
+
+```text
+tau_j -> -omega^(-j)z^-1 + a omega^j z.
+```
+
+The wrapper checks all three identities
+`tau_j(omega z)=tau_(j+1)(z)`, so this repair avoids the invalid one-branch
+mutation.  Multiplying the three factors gives exactly
+
+```text
+product_j (y-tau_j)
+  = y^3 + 3a y + z^-3 - a^3 z^3
+  = y^3 + 3a y + x - a^3 t.
+```
+
+The first forbidden positive coefficient of the purported polynomial input
+`g-c_2` is therefore `-a^3` at `z^3=t^1`.  This is an input-polynomiality
+failure reported at an exact order; the system is rejected before target
+interpolation.  To prevent `a=0` from masquerading as a mutation, the wrapper
+declares
+
+```text
+ring:  QQ[a,sat_witness], lex(a,sat_witness)
+ideal: <a^3, sat_witness*a-1>.
+```
+
+Its Groebner basis is `[1]`, so no nonzero mutation passes that guard.  The
+leading separations, cubic degree leader, and `c=-1` are fixed nonzero units.
+This is `SATURATED-EMPTY` for the covariant input-mutation control only, not
+for a D=105 target or for a completed interpolation ideal.
